WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.488
Picture a small museum with one front door and four gates.

2
00:00:06.488 --> 00:00:09.165
On the door hangs a sign, OPEN or CLOSED.

3
00:00:09.165 --> 00:00:12.235
Beside it, a board shows today's visitor count.

4
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:16.694
The guards have one rule: never trust your memory, always read the board.

5
00:00:16.694 --> 00:00:22.385
When someone comes in, the guard at that gate reads the count, and writes the number one higher.

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.985
The museum's program works the same way.

7
00:00:24.985 --> 00:00:28.961
Four threads, one per gate, share two fields, open and visitors.

8
00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:32.440
Both are volatile, the keyword that fixed the stop loop.

9
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.494
And volatile does its job well.

10
00:00:34.494 --> 00:00:38.473
Every read is fresh: no thread keeps an old copy on its notepad.

11
00:00:38.473 --> 00:00:40.773
And every write is visible, in order.

12
00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:46.865
Switch on the lights before you turn the sign to OPEN, and whoever reads OPEN finds the lights on.

13
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:50.301
So here's a question that feels too obvious to ask.

14
00:00:50.301 --> 00:00:55.334
If every read is fresh and every write is visible, the count has to be right.

15
00:00:55.334 --> 00:00:56.053
Doesn't it?

16
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:57.617
Let's test it.

17
00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:00.945
Four gate threads each let in 1,000,000 visitors.

18
00:01:00.945 --> 00:01:02.574
That's 4,000,000 in all.

19
00:01:02.574 --> 00:01:06.037
What does the volatile counter say at closing time?

20
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:11.096
In one run: 3,348,221.

21
00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:18.090
About 650,000 visitors walked in, and the board never heard of them.

22
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.250
No error, no warning.

23
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.513
Just a smaller number.

24
00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:27.763
Let's slow it down to two visitors and two guards, with the count at 41.

25
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:30.338
Gate 1 reads the board: 41.

26
00:01:30.338 --> 00:01:33.268
Gate 2 reads the board: also 41.

27
00:01:33.268 --> 00:01:36.106
Gate 1 adds one, and writes 42.

28
00:01:36.106 --> 00:01:38.944
Gate 2 adds one, and writes 42.

29
00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:42.177
Two visitors came in, and the count moved by one.

30
00:01:42.177 --> 00:01:46.430
Every one of those four steps was a perfect volatile read or write.

31
00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:50.112
And a guard who comes along later reads 42 and carries on.

32
00:01:50.112 --> 00:01:53.984
Nothing in the program ever learns that someone went missing.

33
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:55.978
Here's what we got wrong.

34
00:01:55.978 --> 00:01:59.111
volatile makes the read fresh, and the write visible.

35
00:01:59.111 --> 00:02:01.652
It does nothing about the gap between them.

36
00:02:01.652 --> 00:02:06.559
In that gap, another guard can walk up, read the same number, and write over yours.

37
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:08.573
This is called a lost update.

38
00:02:08.573 --> 00:02:13.110
visitors++ looks like one step, but it's three: read, add one, write.

39
00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.844
That's a read-modify-write, and volatile never joins its steps together.

40
00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:20.475
Here's the full run.

41
00:02:20.475 --> 00:02:26.106
The plain counter, with no volatile at all, says 3,987,596.

42
00:02:26.106 --> 00:02:26.774
Closer!

43
00:02:26.774 --> 00:02:29.160
Shall we give it a medal?

44
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:29.446
No.

45
00:02:29.446 --> 00:02:38.990
In a run of the Kotlin version, the plain counter came out exact, and the volatile one lost 985,479.

46
00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:43.189
Both are broken, just in different patterns.

47
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.814
What the museum needs is a clicker bolted to the board.

48
00:02:47.814 --> 00:02:52.543
One press reads the count, adds one, and writes it back, as a single move.

49
00:02:52.543 --> 00:02:58.549
Nobody can press it halfway, so a second guard goes before you or after you, never in between.

50
00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:03.756
In Java, that clicker is an AtomicInteger, and incrementAndGet is the press.

51
00:03:03.756 --> 00:03:08.577
It's atomic: other threads see it either not started or completely done.

52
00:03:08.577 --> 00:03:11.657
In the same run, it counted exactly 4,000,000.

53
00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:16.585
For a busy counter you only read now and then, a LongAdder is faster still.

54
00:03:16.585 --> 00:03:21.403
It spreads the additions over several cells, and adds them up when you ask.

55
00:03:21.403 --> 00:03:22.880
Also exactly 4,000,000.

56
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.781
The same trap hides in any group of steps that must happen together.

57
00:03:27.781 --> 00:03:31.594
Check, then act: if the balance is big enough, take the money.

58
00:03:31.594 --> 00:03:33.685
Or keep the larger of two numbers.

59
00:03:33.685 --> 00:03:37.067
Each one reads, decides, and writes, as separate steps.

60
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.315
Make every field volatile, and the museum can still sell the last seat on the evening tour twice.

61
00:03:43.315 --> 00:03:46.152
One happy visitor, and one very confused one.

62
00:03:46.867 --> 00:03:48.573
So when is volatile enough?

63
00:03:48.573 --> 00:03:53.754
When one thread writes the field, and the new value doesn't depend on the old one.

64
00:03:53.754 --> 00:04:00.072
The sign on the door is like that: one guard flips it, and nobody works out OPEN from the old value.

65
00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:03.105
Many writers updating one number need an atomic.

66
00:04:03.105 --> 00:04:05.759
A rule across several fields needs a lock.

67
00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:10.958
Here's the counter in code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

68
00:04:10.958 --> 00:04:14.864
One AtomicInteger, and one incrementAndGet for each visitor.

69
00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:18.783
So, if every read is fresh, why is the count wrong?

70
00:04:18.783 --> 00:04:21.368
Because fresh isn't the same as together.

71
00:04:21.368 --> 00:04:24.395
volatile makes each read and each write visible.

72
00:04:24.395 --> 00:04:26.665
It never makes two of them one step.

73
00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:27.827
Quick check.

74
00:04:27.827 --> 00:04:33.659
Every gate thread runs max = Math.max(max, x) on a volatile field.

75
00:04:33.659 --> 00:04:35.427
Can it lose a value?
